<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hill Complex (Great Zimbabwe)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–<a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hill Complex is the oldest part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>, a group of stone enclosures built among the granite boulders of a steep hill overlooking the valley. Construction began in the 11th or 12th century, and the complex is thought to have been the residence of the kings and a ritual centre. The soapstone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> Birds were found there.</p></div>
